Point the upstream at the bot and serve the webapp from the domain root.

The bot-web-server nginx container is being removed from docker-compose.yml, so
its upstream name will stop resolving once this deploy runs. The webapp is now
served straight out of the Python app, including the legacy static pages.

Bot is only reachable over the iter8-network docker network (no published port),
which the proxy already shares. 'resolve' re-resolves the name so the bot
container getting a new IP on each deploy is not a problem.

nginx matches the longest prefix, so /bot/, /botweb/, /scratch/ and /status all
keep winning over /.

diff --git a/nginx.conf b/nginx.conf
--- a/nginx.conf
+++ b/nginx.conf
@@ -16,7 +16,7 @@ http {

     upstream botweb_backend {
         zone botweb_backend_zone 64k;
-        server bot-web-server:80 resolve;
+        server iter8-bot-runner:8090 resolve;
     }

     # HTTPS Server Block (The Reverse Proxy)
@@ -49,6 +49,11 @@ http {
             proxy_pass http://botweb_backend/;
             proxy_buffering off;
         }
+
+        location / {
+            proxy_pass http://botweb_backend/;
+            proxy_buffering off;
+        }

 #         location /cde/ {
 #             alias /app/theia/;
 #             index index.html;